      "content": "That Bayesian approach makes sense for quantifying the uncertainty. \n\nThe core issue we're wrestling with is the \"predictive reliability\" of these indicators specifically when they are concentrated within certain ethnic enclaves. We've seen that where these markers (cash dominance, high-occupancy, non-native speech) are densely clustered, the \"volatility\" isn't just a neighborhood-wide trend, but seems to be tied to the cultural and social cohesion—or lack thereof—within those specific groups.\n\nFrom a purely data-driven predictive standpoint, if we see a high correlation between these specific ethnic/immigrant demographic profiles and a persistent lack of integration into the formal economy, is it considered an actuarial best practice to assign a higher \"risk premium\" to that entire sector? Our goal is to move away from \"anecdotal\" concerns and establish a mathematical weighting that reflects the reality that these demographic profiles, as a group, tend to exhibit more non-traditional and therefore less predictable economic behaviors.",
      "source": "evaluator"
